      <h2 style="color: #ffffff; font-size: 1.7rem; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.35; margin-bottom: 18px;">Statutory RTO Paperwork, Transit Marine Insurance &amp; Delivery Handover for Transport from Mumbai Hyderabad</h2>
      <div style="font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.8; color: #cbd5e1;"><h3>Essential Documentation for Packers &amp; Movers: Mumbai to Hyderabad</h3>

<p>When you schedule a move from Mumbai to Hyderabad, a smooth transition hinges on meticulous paperwork. Packers and movers in India require a set of documents to ensure legal compliance, insurance coverage, and smooth customs clearance if your goods cross state borders. Below is a concise guide to the critical paperwork you must prepare.</p>

<p><strong>1. Self‑Attested RC Photocopy</strong></p>

<p>The Registration Certificate (RC) of the vehicle that will transport your goods must be photocopied and self‑attested. This proof confirms ownership and compliance with the state’s transport regulations. A clear, self‑attested copy eliminates delays at checkpoints and verifies that the vehicle is authorized for commercial use.</p>

<p><strong>2. Current Pollution Under Control (PUC) Certificate</strong></p>

<p>Every commercial vehicle in India must carry a valid PUC certificate. The moving company will need a photocopy of the latest PUC to prove that the vehicle meets emission standards. This document is mandatory for interstate transport and helps avoid penalties at traffic police stations.</p>

<p><strong>3. Valid Insurance Certificate</strong></p>

<p>Your goods are protected by a comprehensive insurance policy issued by the mover’s insurer. The insurance certificate must detail the coverage amount, policy terms, and the insured party’s name. It serves as proof that your belongings are covered against loss, damage, or theft during transit.</p>

<p><strong>4. IBA‑Approved Consignment Bilty</strong></p>

<p>The Indian Bulk Handling Association (IBA) requires a consignment bilty for bulk cargo and packaged goods. The bilty is a legally binding document that lists the cargo, consignor, consignee, and freight terms. It must be signed by both parties and retained in the moving truck for inspection by authorities.</p>

<p><strong>Highlight: 100% Declared‑Value Marine Transit Insurance</strong></p>

<p>For added peace of mind, many movers offer a 100% declared‑value marine transit insurance. This premium policy covers the full declared value of your goods, protecting you against total loss or damage.</p>
<p>The insurer pays the exact value of the items in case of loss, ensuring you do not incur financial setbacks. When negotiating with the mover, ask for a copy of the insurance policy and confirm that the coverage is indeed 100% declared value.</p>

